---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

import checkout from '../../../assets/case_study_images/supergood/checkout.png';
import dishSheetDark from '../../../assets/case_study_images/supergood/dish-sheet-dark.png';
import dishSheetLight from '../../../assets/case_study_images/supergood/dish-sheet-light.png';
import history from '../../../assets/case_study_images/supergood/history.png';
import { sitePath } from '../../../config/site';
import BaseDocument from '../../../layouts/BaseDocument.astro';
import '../../../styles/case-study.css';

const heroTitle = 'SuperGood';
---

<BaseDocument
  title="SuperGood case study | Igor Kupchinenko"
  description="How I designed and built SuperGood’s ordering experience, from choosing a meal to ordering it again."
  pageClass="supergood-case-study editorial-case-study"
  ogImage={dishSheetLight.src}
>


  <main id="main-content">
    <header class="hero">

      <h1 class="hero-title">{heroTitle}</h1>
      <p class="hero__line">Homemade food, without making the order feel like a gamble.</p>
      <p class="hero__intro">
        I joined PizzaSushiWok as a mobile engineer. Its homemade-food delivery app, SuperGood, was drawing complaints about the app and whether the food was worth the price. I took on the order experience, from design to the Flutter build.
      </p>

      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Product direction, research, UX/UI, Flutter</dd></div>
        <div><dt>When</dt><dd>2024, at PizzaSushiWok</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Research</dt><dd>Customer research and prototype tasks</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>Shipped on Android and iOS. Later closed for financial reasons.</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <figure class="hero-phones">
        <Image
          src={dishSheetLight}
          alt="Working SuperGood dish sheet with photo, price, nutrition, ingredients, and add-to-cart."
          widths={[320, 480, 640]}
          sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc((100vw - 60px) / 3), 220px"
          format="webp"
          loading="eager"
          decoding="async"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
        <Image
          src={checkout}
          alt="Working SuperGood checkout with address, delivery time, payment, bonuses, and order button."
          widths={[320, 480, 640]}
          sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc((100vw - 60px) / 3), 220px"
          format="webp"
          loading="eager"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <Image
          src={history}
          alt="Working SuperGood order history with expandable cards and repeat-order actions."
          widths={[320, 480, 597]}
          sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc((100vw - 60px) / 3), 220px"
          format="webp"
          loading="eager"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Dish details, checkout, and order history from the shipped app.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </header>

    <section class="chapter" id="context" data-index-section>

      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2>What makes this meal worth ordering?</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Lunch at work. Dinner when cooking feels like too much. SuperGood competed with Кухня на районе for those everyday orders, promising homemade-style food without a long wait.</p>
          <p>But public reviews questioned the value of the food as well as the app. I could change the ordering experience; kitchens, recipes, pricing, and courier operations were outside my role.</p>
          <p>Before someone paid, the app needed to make the choice clear: what they were getting, what it would cost, and when it would arrive.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="research" data-index-section>

      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2>The decision continued after choosing a dish.</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Customer conversations, observing people order, and prototype tasks kept bringing me back to the same concerns. A photo did not tell people enough about a dish. They checked delivery time and total before paying. And weekday orders often repeated.</p>
          <p>I also studied nearby services. Кухня на районе offered a useful comparison for the menu and delivery promise. Самокат showed ways to handle address selection, item quantities, and the basket while browsing.</p>
          <p>I used comparison tests and polls as well, but I do not have reliable sample sizes or win rates to report. The decisions below describe how I responded to the observations.</p>
          <p id="direction">I was rebuilding the separate Android and iOS apps in Flutter. That let me carry the same ordering behaviour across both platforms, from the first dish to the next visit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter chapter--wide" id="solution" data-index-section>

      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2>From “what’s in it?” to “the same again.”</h2>
        <p class="wide-copy">
          I focused on the moments when someone needed more information, changed their order, or returned for a meal they already knew.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="path-group">
        <ol class="journey-map">
          <li id="dish">
            <h3>A photo can only tell you so much.</h3>
            <p class="journey-map__decision">I put weight, ingredients, nutrition, and extra cooking time on the dish sheet, with the price beside Add. People could inspect what they were buying before putting it in the basket.</p>
            <figure>
              <Image src={dishSheetLight} alt="Working dish sheet with photo, weight, price, nutrition, ingredients, and add to cart." widths={[360, 480, 640]} sizes="(max-width: 340px) calc(100vw - 40px), 300px" format="webp" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

            </figure>
          </li>
          <li id="basket">
            <h3>Add a dish. Keep track of the order.</h3>
            <p class="journey-map__decision">I put quantity controls on the item and kept delivery time, item count, and total in a persistent order bar. Changing a quantity no longer required a trip away from the menu.</p>
            <figure>
              <Image src={dishSheetDark} alt="Working dark dish sheet with a persistent order bar showing delivery time, count, and total." widths={[360, 480, 640]} sizes="(max-width: 340px) calc(100vw - 40px), 300px" format="webp" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

            </figure>
          </li>
          <li id="pay">
            <h3>One place to check before paying.</h3>
            <p class="journey-map__decision">At checkout, I brought address, delivery time, payment, bonuses, and total together. Each could be changed in place while keeping the order intact.</p>
            <figure>
              <Image src={checkout} alt="Working checkout with address, delivery time, payment, bonuses, and order button." widths={[360, 480, 640]} sizes="(max-width: 340px) calc(100vw - 40px), 300px" format="webp" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

            </figure>
          </li>
          <li id="history">
            <h3>You already know what you want.</h3>
            <p class="journey-map__decision">Weekday meals often repeated. I made history a way to order again: expand an earlier order, add individual dishes, or bring back the whole basket. Favourites offered another route to familiar meals.</p>
            <figure>
              <Image src={history} alt="Working order history with expandable cards, repeat buttons, and add whole order." widths={[360, 480, 597]} sizes="(max-width: 340px) calc(100vw - 40px), 300px" format="webp" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

            </figure>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="validation-board">
        <article class="validation-case">
          <p>The screens shown here survive from production. The earlier app and prototypes were not preserved, so there is no visual before-and-after comparison.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter chapter--wide chapter--outcome" id="outcome" data-index-section>

      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2>The app shipped on Android and iOS.</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>I designed and built the Flutter app, replacing the two separate applications. My work covered first run, menu and basket, payment and 3DS, maps and tracking, favourites and history, promotions and loyalty, themes, and adaptive layouts, followed by usability and reliability fixes.</p>
          <p>SuperGood later closed because of the company’s financial situation. The redesign reached production, but I do not have permissioned conversion, retention, or repeat-order metrics to report its effect.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter reflection" id="reflection" data-index-section>

      <div class="chapter__body">
        <h2>Did a clearer order lead to another one?</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The redesign gave people more information before paying and a shorter route back to a familiar meal. Whether those changes made them more likely to order again is the question I would want to measure.</p>
          <p>I would start with repeat-order completion, then look at whether clearer dish details and delivery time reduced the “not worth it” complaint.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  <footer class="case-footer">
    <nav aria-label="SuperGood project links">
      <a href={sitePath('/')}>Home</a>
      <a href={sitePath('/case-studies/two-sticks/')}>Two Sticks case study →</a>
      <a href={sitePath('/case-studies/observatory/')}>Observatory case study →</a>
    </nav>
  </footer>
</BaseDocument>
